<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Chester</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 615</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> was fought around 615 near Chester, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith of Northumbria</a> defeated the Britons of Powys and their allies. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, Æthelfrith slaughtered many monks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangor-on-dee">Bangor-on-Dee</a> who had come to pray for the British army. The Welsh king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selyf-ap-cynan">Selyf ap Cynan</a> of Powys was among the dead, and the victory weakened the links between the Britons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and those of the north.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The reasons for the battle are unknown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-of-monmouth">Geoffrey of Monmouth</a> states that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith</a>'s political rival, Edwin of Deira, was living in exile in Gwynedd, and there are some supporting references to Edwin in Reginald of Durham and the Welsh Triads, but no evidence that Æthelfrith was pursuing him. It seems unlikely that he was protecting Anglian settlers in the area, and he may have been planning to attack Powys. The annals and chronicles give a variety of years for the battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith</a> destroyed a combined force from Powys and Rhôs, a cantref of the Kingdom of Gwynedd, and possibly from Mercia. Cadwal Crysban of Rhôs and Gwion ap Cyndrwyn of Pengwern were among the dead, and circumstantial evidence suggests that King Iago of Gwynedd may have been killed too. The Brut y Brenhinedd, the Middle Welsh versions of Geoffrey's history, calls the battle Perllan Fangor, Bangor Orchard, which suggests it may have been fought nearer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangor-on-dee">Bangor-on-Dee</a>.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith</a> ordered the praying monks to be attacked first, saying that though they bore no arms they fought against him by their prayers, and about twelve hundred are said to have been killed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> speaks of 200 priests slain and says their leader, Scrocmail, escaped as one of fifty. The attack on the monks before the army may perhaps be explained as a ruse to throw the defence into disarray.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Excavations at Heronbridge, just south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a>, in 2004 found post-Roman graves beneath a defensive earthwork, and there is evidence that they hold casualties of the battle. Its strategic significance remains unclear, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith</a> died soon afterwards, at the Battle of the River Idle according to some accounts. It has been suggested that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cearl-of-mercia">Cearl of Mercia</a> shared in the Britons' defeat, as his overkingship ended until the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> saw the massacre as divine retribution for the Welsh bishops' refusal to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-canterbury">Augustine of Canterbury</a> in converting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>. During the English Reformation scholars such as Matthew Parker argued that Augustine himself had been complicit, relying on an early date in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>. The charge was rejected by scholars such as Elizabeth Elstob, Henry Spelman, Henry Wharton and Jeremy Collier, and it has no support from modern historians, not least because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith</a> and his army were pagan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Chester” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They were first joined around 604 by Æthelfrith of Bernicia, who defeated Áedán mac Gabráin of Dál Riata at the Battle of Degsastan in 603 and the Britons near <b>Chester</b> about 615.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 615 Æthelfrith of Northumbria defeated the Britons at the <b>Battle of Chester</b>, where King Selyf ap Cynan of Powys was killed and, according to Bede, about 1,200 monks of Bangor-on-Dee who had come to pray for the British army were slaughtered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late 1st century three legions remained, at Caerleon, <b>Chester</b> and York, with auxiliary forts across Wales and the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Aethelfrith defeated Aedan of Dal Riata at Degsastan in 603 and the Britons at <b>Chester</b>, then seized Deira.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bernicia">Kingdom of Bernicia</a></li></ul></section>
